  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1201 AM CDT Mon Oct 12 2026\n\nToday\n\nSouth and southwest winds ahead of an approaching cold front\nwill continue to bring well-above normal temperatures into the\nregion. 850 mb temperatures will range from 15 to 20C. These \nare 2.5 to 3.2 standard deviations above-normal in the NAEFS \nand ECMWF ESAT. As a result, we will likely see another day with\nnear-record and record high temperatures in the 80s.\n\nAs warm air and moisture advection moves into areas along and\nnorth of Interstate 90, the CAMs are indicating that there may \nbe a band of showers during the afternoon. If they did happen to\noccur, rainfall amounts would range from a trace to a tenth of \nan inch. \n\nTonight\n\nA cold front will move through the area. The NBM continues to \nindicate a 30 to 60 percent chance of showers along and north \nof Interstate 94. The CAMs are trending drier and drier as the \nbetter forcing and 850 mb moisture transport is more to our \nnorth and northeast. \n\nWith the front not reaching the Interstate 90 corridor until\naround 1 AM, there will be potential for some daily warm records\nto be either tied or broken for October 12. Low temperatures by\nsunrise on Tuesday will range from the mid-50s to mid-60s.\n\nTuesday Night into Wednesday Night\n\nA low pressure system moves northeast along the cold front to\nour south. Some of this moisture is streaming north from the\nremnants of Hurricane Simon. Like the past couple of days, the\nGFS is a bit further north than the ECMWF with this front. As a\nresult, there remains much uncertainty on whether any of this\nrain will be able to get as far north as northeast Iowa and\nsouthwest Wisconsin. \n\nHigh temperatures will be in the 60s on Wednesday. Low\ntemperatures will range from the upper 30s to lower 50s.\n\nThursday into Friday\n\nThe models are indicating that there will be some phasing of \nnorthern and southern stream shortwave troughs over the Upper\nand Mid Mississippi River Valleys. A surface low will track to\nour south and southeast. Meanwhile, the northern stream\nshortwave will bring some precipitation northward into our area.\nThis precipitation will be on the light side with the\nprobabilities of precipitation totals greater than a tenth of \nan inch less than 30 percent. \n\nIn the wake of this trough, colder air will move into the area.\nHigh temperatures on Friday will be in the 50s and range from \nthe mid-20s to mid-30s on Friday night. This could bring an end \nto the growing season for some areas.",
